  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 220 AM Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Typical late-summer weather is expected, with \nmostly isolated late-day showers and storms for the next several \ndays, primarily over the NW Piedmont and far SE. \n\nOur mid level flow pattern remains focused on a weak shear axis to \nour W, from AR to Lake Ontario, sandwiched between strong \nanticyclones over the Desert Southwest and off the Southeast coast, \nwhile a large Canadian gyre spins over Manitoba/Ontario into the \nUpper Midwest. The ridge off the Southeast will steadily flatten and \nspread westward over the Southeast/FL through the weekend, limiting \nthe advection of Atlantic/Gulf-source moisture into NC. At the \nsurface, as high pressure sits off the Carolina coast, anticyclonic \nwinds from the SSW and SW will persist over NC through early Mon, \nwith lee troughing holding over the W Piedmont. Over the next few \ndays, the better (but still low) convection chances over central NC \nwill remain in the far SE, associated with the inland-tracking sea \nbreeze, and in the NW, where mountain storms may drift late in the \nday. PW values will hover near normal through Sun, with daily SBCAPE \npeaking around 1000-1500 J/kg, but we'll be lacking large scale \nforcing for ascent or any noteworthy boundaries to focus convection, \nbesides perhaps the sea breeze or an old outflow. Deep layer bulk \nshear will remain poor, just 15-20 kts into early next week, which \nwill limit the organization and longevity of any convection that can \ndevelop. As such, pops should remain below climatology, largely (but \nnot solely) in the far SE and the NW Piedmont. \n\nThe weak trough to our W and NW will pivot clockwise and diminish \nSun into Mon, as the mid level high over/off FL becomes further \nsuppressed and the westerlies dip a bit further into the Midwest and \nGreat Lakes region. As a result, our mid level flow will veer to \nmore westerly or WNW and remain weak. Periods of slightly better \nshower/storm chances start to pop up starting late Mon, when some \nmodels show a weak perturbation (maybe an MCV) tracking from the \nMidwest ESE through NC, bringing modest dynamic lift and an uptick \nin PW especially across our SE half, where 25-50% pops are expected. \nOtherwise, pops are likely to stay just under climatology until Thu, \nwhen the faster northern stream flow digs broadly over the \nMidwest/Great Lakes then into the Mid Atlantic region. This should \nhelp support greater and more organized convection. We may even end \nup within a train of Midwest waves and MCVs tracking ESE into the \nregion, that might briefly boost pops late in the week. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Above-normal temperatures expected through much of \nnext week, with the heat peaking Tue-Wed when highs will be in the \nlow-mid 90s.\n\nAs the flattening mid level ridge steadily expands westward over the \nSoutheast and Gulf states starting early next week, surface lee \ntroughing will sharpen over the Piedmont, with a downturn in cloud \ncover leading to greater warming at the surface, while mid level \nlapse rates remain modest to low. Low level thicknesses are expected \nto rise to 5-15 m above normal starting Sun, when highs should reach \n90 or higher most places. The hottest days of this stretch are \nexpected to be Tue/Wed, when highs will be in the low-mid 90s, with \nlows in the low-mid 70s. The NWS experimental Heat Risk does reach \nthe Major category in some areas then, suggesting a higher chance of \nheat-related illnesses for those without adequate cooling. By late \nweek, as the northern stream dips into the Mid Atlantic with an \napproaching cold front from the NW and N, the resulting uptick in \nclouds and pop coverage should help nudge highs down a bit by Fri.",
